---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { codeTheme, gsapCode } from '../../content/examples';
import Documentation from '../../layouts/Documentation.astro';
---
<Documentation>
  <p>Install the browser package, optional <code>@domfx/gsap</code> artifact and GSAP peer using the <a href="/docs/installation#add-the-optional-gsap-binding">local setup</a>. The binding drives a manual attachment; the app owns its timeline.</p>
  <h2 id="provide-your-elements">Provide your elements</h2>
  <p>Run after mount. In <code>main.ts</code>, find the elements from the preview HTML:</p>
  <div class="docs-code-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Find the target and host"><Code code={`const host = document.querySelector('#host');
const target = document.querySelector('#target');
if (!(host instanceof HTMLElement) || !target) {
  throw new Error('Missing host or target');
}`} lang="ts" theme={codeTheme} /></div>
  <p>To use your own heading, replace the preview button with <code>&lt;h1 id="target"&gt;Your heading&lt;/h1&gt;</code> inside the positioned host. Keep the heading's text selectable; the effect uses a separate decorative canvas.</p>
  <h2 id="build-your-sequence">Build your sequence</h2>
  <p>Add this to the same module. The host fades in over half a second; Fire then moves from time 0 to 4 and intensity 0.8 to 0.4 over four seconds. Fade the host so the original target and its effect share opacity. Fading the target alone is unsupported composition.</p>
  <div class="docs-code-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Full manual attachment and GSAP lifecycle example"><Code code={gsapCode} lang="ts" theme={codeTheme} /></div>
  <h2 id="seek-and-play">Seek and play</h2>
  <p>Use <code>timeline.play()</code>, <code>pause()</code>, <code>seek()</code> or <code>reverse()</code>. At <code>timeline.seek(2.5)</code>, Fire has time 2 and intensity 0.6. Seeking backward to 1.5 gives time 1 and intensity 0.7. Default callback-suppressed seeking still updates the controls.</p>
  <p><code>timeScale()</code> changes playback speed; timeline <code>repeat</code> and <code>yoyo</code> control repetition. Use one owner for Fire's controls: later direct writes do not redefine the binding's captured starting values.</p>
  <h2 id="own-the-lifetime">Own the lifetime</h2>
  <p>Call <code>cleanup()</code> on component unmount. It disposes this binding, this example's owned timeline and the attachment. Registration before <code>ready</code> settles also covers navigation during initialization.</p>
  <p><code>binding.dispose()</code> alone removes its child tween, preserving caller-owned siblings and the last controls. When borrowing a timeline, leave timeline cleanup with its owner.</p>
  <p>For <code>gsap.context()</code> cleanup, use <code>context.revert()</code>. An asynchronous attachment can use <code>bindFireAsync()</code> with explicit <code>disposeEffect: true</code> ownership to dispose a result arriving after teardown.</p>
  <h2 id="unavailable-and-retry">Unavailable and retry</h2>
  <p>The timeline starts only when Fire is active. Missing GPU support or reduced motion leaves it paused and preserves the original target. Correct the condition, then await <code>fire.retry()</code> and inspect its lifecycle. Retry retains the existing binding and controls; do not add another binding.</p>
  <p>See <a href="/docs/api">API essentials</a> and <a href="/docs/troubleshooting">troubleshooting</a> for the shared attachment contract.</p>
</Documentation>
